// Standing regression fixture for ARC Task 6 review round 2 (reviewer
// Critical): a global's OWN Init expression never routed through
// lowCountedStore's retain-on-store -- cpEmitGlobalsInit's Init-emission
// path plain-assigned a bare-alias Init's handle with NO retain
// (`cv_g2 = cv_g1;`), so Task 6's new unconditional cl_free_globals
// double-released an rc==1 handle: reviewer-reproduced `rt_rc:
// over-release`, exit 134, under CLARUS_MEM_STRICT+CLARUS_MEM_PARANOID.
// Fixed by giving the Init path its own birth-vs-retain classification
// (irGlobalInitBirth, set by lowGlobalVarDecl using the SAME
// lowStoreIsBirth/lowStoreIntrOwnsResult rule a runtime store uses) and
// retaining a non-birth Init's value right after the plain-assign --
// cpEmitGlobalsInit's own comment (cprint.cla) has the full account.
//
// gaT1/gaT2 pin the reviewer's exact TEXT repro (`var g2: text = g1`).
// gaL1/gaL2 pin the LIST-OF-TEXT variant (`var g2: list of text = g1`) --
// same missing-retain bug, one level up: the retain is on the LIST
// CONTAINER's own handle (cpEmitRetain's KList branch), not its elements.
//
// gaL1 is emptied again (`pop()`) before the function returns, so the
// shared list is EMPTY by the time cl_free_globals runs at exit -- this is
// deliberate, not incidental: a SEPARATE, pre-existing gap (cpEmitRelease's
// deep element-release walk runs unconditionally on EVERY release
// call-site, never gated on the container's own refcount actually reaching
// 0) means two globals that still share a list/map handle WITH elements
// present AT EXIT double-release those elements -- confirmed reproducible
// (both via an alias Init and via a plain runtime `g2 = g1` reassignment)
// during this fix's own verification, but it is a DIFFERENT bug class (an
// unconditional deep-walk on every release call-site, not a missing
// retain) and NOT fixed by this task -- see task-6-report.md's fix-round-2
// addendum for the full writeup and reproduction. This fixture stays
// entirely inside the currently-safe "empty at exit" envelope while still
// proving the CONTAINER-level alias is real: `gaL2.push`-visible-via-
// `gaL1` (and vice versa) is exactly Ch3's documented reference semantics
// ("all references to the same handle point to the same underlying data"),
// which only holds if the Init actually shared the handle rather than
// silently copying an independent empty list.
//
// Expected strict-mode leak count: 0 (see ../arc_global_alias.leaks --
// absent, defaults to want-0). Both pairs are fully balanced: one retain
// at the alias Init, two matching releases at exit (rc 1 -> 2 -> 1 -> 0 for
// each pair, never touching 0 early and never double-freeing).

var gaT1: text = "hello"
var gaT2: text = gaT1

var gaL1: list of text
var gaL2: list of text = gaL1

func runArcGlobalAlias() {
    var buf: char[64]
    var n: int
    var s: string
    var popped: text

    n = gaT1.toBytes(buf)
    s.fromBytes(buf, n)
    alert(s)

    n = gaT2.toBytes(buf)
    s.fromBytes(buf, n)
    alert(s)

    gaL1.push("shared")
    if gaL2.count == 1 and gaL2.first() == "shared" {
        alert("list-alias-ok")
    } else {
        alert("list-alias-bad")
    }
    popped = gaL1.pop()
    n = popped.toBytes(buf)
    s.fromBytes(buf, n)
    alert(s)
}
